      "page_idx": 13,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.815,
            0.049,
            0.861,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": "Area"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.09,
            0.332,
            0.113
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Parallelogram"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.121,
            0.846,
            0.161
          ],
          "content": "We can derive a special formula for the area of a parallelogram by converting it into a rectangle of equal area."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.17,
            0.844,
            0.214
          ],
          "content": "？ Give a method to convert a parallelogram into a rectangle of equal area. You can try this using a cut-out of a parallelogram."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.214,
            0.845,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "Construct AX perpendicular to CD — represented in short as $AX  \\perp  CD$ We call this a **height** of the parallelogram. Cut the parallelogram into ∆AXD and trapezium ABCX."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.273,
            0.459,
            0.386
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.508,
            0.273,
            0.772,
            0.419
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.424,
            0.846,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": "? Can ∆AXD and ABCX fit together, as shown in the figure, to get a rectangle?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.45,
            0.845,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": "One simple way to check this is to identify the triangle that can complete ABCX to a rectangle, and then check if this triangle is congruent to ∆AXD."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.314,
            0.493,
            0.668,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.616,
            0.847,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "Observe that $\\angle X = 90^{\\circ}$ , and so $\\angle A = 90^{\\circ}$ , since AB‖XC. We need another right angle to get a rectangle (what about the fourth angle?). To get the third right angle, extend XC to the right and then construct a line perpendicular to XC that passes through B. The ∆BYC completes ABCX to a rectangle. Is ∆AXD congruent to it?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.335,
            0.713,
            0.676,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": "BY = AX (since ABYX is a rectangle)"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.337,
            0.732,
            0.528,
            0.749
          ],
          "content": "\\angle BTC = \\angle AAD = 90^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.335,
            0.75,
            0.726,
            0.769
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{BC} = \\mathrm{AD} (  since ABCD is a parallellogram  )",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.773,
            0.847,
            0.83
          ],
          "content": "So, by the RHS congruency criterion, $\\Delta \\mathrm{BYC} \\cong \\Delta \\mathrm{AXD}$ . Thus, ∆AXD will fit exactly over the region occupied by ∆BYC, and convert the parallelogram into a rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.834,
            0.846,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": "The process of cutting a figure into pieces and rearranging them to get a different figure of equal area is called **dissection.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.871,
            0.913,
            0.917,
            0.935
          ],
          "content": "161"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
